    {
      "id": "g02",
      "title": {
        "t": "heißen, kommen – du oder Sie?",
        "s": "heißen, kommen – du or Sie?"
      },
      "explain": "To say your name, use **ich heiße** (from heißen, *to be called*). To ask someone's name: **Wie heißt du?** (informal) or **Wie heißen Sie?** (polite).\nGerman has two words for *you*. Use **du** with family, friends and children. Use **Sie** with adults you don't know, at work, in offices, and with **Frau** / **Herr** + family name.\nTo ask where someone is from: **Woher kommst du?** / **Woher kommen Sie?** – **Ich komme aus Deutschland.** Most countries have no article, but a few do: **aus dem Iran**, **aus der Türkei**. Just learn these as fixed phrases.\nIn a question with a question word (**wie**, **woher**, **wer**), the verb comes second: **Woher kommen Sie?**",
      "table": [
        [
          "ich",
          "heiße",
          "komme"
        ],
        [
          "du",
          "heißt",
          "kommst"
        ],
        [
          "er / sie / es",
          "heißt",
          "kommt"
        ],
        [
          "Sie",
          "heißen",
          "kommen"
        ]
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "Wie heißt du? – Ich heiße Amir.",
          "s": "What's your name? – My name is Amir."
        },
        {
          "t": "Wie heißen Sie? – Ich heiße Eva Müller.",
          "s": "What's your name? – My name is Eva Müller."
        },
        {
          "t": "Woher kommst du? – Ich komme aus dem Iran.",
          "s": "Where are you from? – I'm from Iran."
        },
        {
          "t": "Woher kommen Sie, Herr Schmidt?",
          "s": "Where are you from, Mr. Schmidt?"
        },
        {
          "t": "Lukas kommt aus Berlin.",
          "s": "Lukas is from Berlin."
        }
      ],
